%I A399781 #26 Oct 13 2026 20:45:08
%S A399781 12,99,604,6477,2526,35123,63120,39351,104668,705063,512832,1123291,
%T A399781 4357794,4942725,4809002,5922333,32995074,105484499,37212132,48150981,
%U A399781 248308808,598559985,789565560,864056597,1913815518,2311939605,2662143136,359589591,5527105182,9042022067
%N A399781 a(n) is the average of the lower and upper end of the earliest prime gap of length 2*n that does not contain a semiprime A001358.
%C A399781 a(n) > 2.1*10^13 for n = 55..57, 62, 63 and n >= 65; a(58) = 8139024767625, a(59) = 18803575292298, a(60) = 17736001201189, a(61) = 10533189055092, a(64) = 3141990247743.
%C A399781 See Terry Tao's answer in the linked Mathoverflow discussion regarding the question of whether this sequence could be finite.
%C A399781 Empirically, log(a(n)) ~ 4.1sqrt(n) for 5 <= n <= 54 (least-squares fit 4.047n^0.5046, rms residual 0.72), i.e., the earliest semiprime-free prime gap of length g occurs near x with g ~ 0.12*(log(x))^2. The known terms with n > 54 lie below this fit, a(64) by a factor of about 70; for n > 54 the known terms are a biased sample, since only values below the search limit can be observed. A Cramér-type probabilistic model suggests that semiprime-free gaps of every even length exist, so that the sequence is infinite with log(a(n)) of order sqrt(n), possibly up to a slowly varying factor involving log(log(a(n))). A proof appears to be out of reach because of the parity problem of sieve theory; see Tao's answer in the Mathoverflow link.
%H A399781 Hugo Pfoertner, <a href="/A399781/b399781.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..54</a>
%H A399781 Harald Cramér, <a href="https://eudml.org/doc/205441">On the order of magnitude of the difference between consecutive prime numbers</a>, Acta Arithmetica 2.1 (1936): 23-46.
%H A399781 Andrew Granville, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1080/03461238.1995.10413946">Harald Cramér and the distribution of prime numbers</a>. Scandinavian Actuarial Journal, 1995(1), 12-28.
%H A399781 Nilotpal Kanti Sinha, <a href="https://mathoverflow.net/questions/414186/are-there-highly-composite-prime-gaps">Are there highly composite prime gaps?</a> Question in mathoverflow, with an answer by Terry Tao, Jan 19 2022.
%F A399781 a(n) = A136344(n) + n.
%F A399781 a(n) >= A000230(n) + n, with equality iff the earliest prime gap of length 2n contains no semiprime.
%o A399781 (PARI) a399781(pmax) = {my(g=vector(ceil(log(pmax)^2)), p1=3, L=List()); forprime(p2=5, pmax, my(d=(p2-p1)/2); if(g[d]==0, my(s=0); for(k=p1+1, p2-1, if(bigomega(k)==2, s=1; break)); if(s==0, g[d]=(p1+p2)/2)); p1=p2); for(k=1, #g, if(g[k]>0, listput(L,g[k]),break)); Vec(L)};
%o A399781 a399781(11*10^7) \\ first 20 terms.
%Y A399781 Cf. A000230, A001358, A002386, A005250, A136344, A359636.
%K A399781 nonn,changed
%O A399781 1,1
